From 8ff79e7621944fafbde09fbebb6055f40bc4a25f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slopdealer <326519127+slopdealer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:16:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/49] R6: output without status lines writes every message
 once, as stock; nothing is queued behind a failed write

runWithoutStatus kept a failed or stalled record at the head of one queue
for both streams and wrote nothing behind it: with stdout on a full disk
no error message reached stderr, and every later message was held in memory
without bound.

The retry queue is removed. Messages for a regular file are still collected
(64 KiB, 100 microseconds idle, one stream at a time, flushed before the
other stream, a status update, Flush and shutdown), so the healthy path is
unchanged. A failed write is reported on stderr and dropped. When a
collected write fails, the rest is written line by line, each line once,
which gives the file content and the reports of stock's one write per line.
Writers that are not regular files get one Write per message with stock's
code. Memory is bounded by one batch.

Tests: status_test.go:207 called Flush on ui.Terminal, which has no such
method, so the package did not build (stock's tests included).
TestRunWithoutStatusKeepsCustomWriterCallsSeparate was right and passes
now. The tests that asserted the retry queue are removed:
TestRunWithoutStatusKeepsSuffixAfterZeroProgressRetry,
TestRunWithoutStatusRetriesPartialBatchWrite, output_failure_order_test.go
and status_output_retry_test.go. status_failing_writer_test.go compares
with stock's behaviour instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
